# FRICTION.log

Running log of everything that costs time while building against Uniswap. One entry per incident,
appended when it happens — this file is the raw material `FEEDBACK.md` is edited down from.

Format:

    YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM  [area]  what I expected -> what actually happened  (cost: Xm)

`area` is the surface, e.g. v4-core, v4-periphery, v3-periphery, quoter, hooks, docs, tooling.
Record the expectation before the outcome; "I assumed X was true because the docs say Y" is the
part that makes the feedback actionable and the part that is impossible to reconstruct later.

---

2026-09-04 09:00  [meta]  log started at D1, per plan.

2026-09-04 18:20  [v3-periphery/v4-core]  pragma pinning, avoided rather than hit.
    Expected to import Uniswap sources directly and compile them alongside the callback.
    Could not: v3 implementations are `=0.7.6`, v4's PoolManager.sol/PositionManager.sol are
    exactly `0.8.26`, and this project follows Midnight at 0.8.34. Wrote test/interfaces/
    IUniswapMinimal.sol by hand instead and bound the already-deployed Base contracts.
    Worth stating precisely in FEEDBACK.md: the cost here was NOT a multi-profile build, because
    fork-testing against a chain where everything is deployed sidesteps it entirely. The cost was
    having to know that workaround exists. It is not in any Uniswap integration doc I found.
    (cost: ~25m, mostly writing interfaces by hand)

2026-09-04 18:35  [docs]  no canonical machine-readable deployment list found for Base.
    Expected an official addresses page/JSON to resolve v3 factory, NonfungiblePositionManager,
    v4 PoolManager and PositionManager on Base. Ended up cross-validating on-chain instead:
    NPM.factory() == v3 factory, PositionManager.poolManager() == PoolManager,
    factory.feeAmountTickSpacing(500) == 10. That worked and is arguably better practice, but it
    is a verification step every integrator repeats from scratch.
    (cost: ~15m)
